WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.000
In previous videos, you could see us dealing with mimosas.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
An invasive tree species that is all over our land.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.640
We cut them, because they are very fire risky.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.640
And, we want to open space for native trees to grow.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.040
The whole region burned down in 2017, 
so we want to become more fire resilient.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.640
Last season, we cleared a big patch behind Basekamp.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.800
And, in the previous episode,
we transformed it into a living area.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.960
Now, we are going to take out a bigger patch!

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.120
This also will help to understand
better how to deal with these trees.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.560
Welcome to a new Project Kamp update!

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.560
So, at the moment, it's getting close to summer, 
which is hot summer again.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.600
Fire risks are coming.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.840
We want to take down many of these ones,
so the view will change drastically.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.240
There are a few reasons we do this.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.360
So, the first reason is this one.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.560
The distance between the buildings and the mimosa.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.880
This is not allowed, so we will need to
clear a whole radius around the house.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.840
The second reason on why we want to clear out
is that we want to make more space.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.560
For fruit trees and native trees to grow.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.960
Which, we have done already in this area.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.120
And, the third reason is that we create
more space, and more openness.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.320
Which means we have more space to relax,
and some airflow during summer.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.280
So, we are now in the electricity line, 
which less than a year ago, it has been cleared.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.200
And, unfortunately, they are already going
back up to two meters high.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.200
As you might know, mimosas are a big topic for us,
and not only for us!

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.640
Many people in the region are struggling with this, because..

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.480
What do you do with such a flammable, 
and a fast growing species on the land?

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:38.880
What to do?

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.880
How do you cut it and how do you process it?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.760
We are going to use our land 
to experiment several options from this.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:48.807
Let's have a look from the top!

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.800
This is the area we are going to work in.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.440
We decided to divide the batch in two, 
and use different approaches to deal with the mimosas.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.320
On this area,
we will debark them with manual tools.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.480
The trees will then die slowly.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.240
It's an experiment to see if this approach makes sense.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.760
Also, for the rest of our land,
and maybe helpful to others with the same problem.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.240
We will also need to clear a smaller area below the electricity lines,
but not likely that we can debark here.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.840
The other side we will fully chop down with chainsaws.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.480
It's a rough, but a more straightforward approach.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.360
It's needed to create a fire security area
around the office before summer comes.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.080
When the risk of fire increases...

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.960
The downside is that the trees will resprout soon, 
but for the moment, we need to take action fast!

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.640
Okay, so, today's the day.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.600
It's very windy, but we are going to start anyway.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.960
We're going to start with the area,
which we're going to strip the bark of the mimosas down.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.880
Let's have a look, if there are some people that join me!

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.520
I think with this team, we can make it!

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.440
Maybe it's a good moment to introduce two more people
of our team, that you already saw in other updates.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.880
I am Marilia! Hi!

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.640
I was happily born and raised in Brazil.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.640
But now, my adventure is here,
on the other side of the ocean.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.360
I came to Europe to study screenwriting.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.964
And, by some mysterious and magical means, I arrived here

00:03:16.964 --> 00:03:20.400
As a writer, I'm loving this experience.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.560
It is so different from the days
I spend alone behind my computer.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.640
Also, as a person in search of meaning, 
this project and these people have been a beautiful gift!

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.639
Hi! I'm Sean!

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:39.200
I'm 37, from the United Kingdom!

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.040
I work as a freelance software developer,
and I'm passionate about environmental issues and trance music.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.040
I came to Project Camp to experience living in a community.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.520
And, to work in nature, for learning new skills 
completely different to what I do back home.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.320
By just typing away on a keyboard.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.320
I don't really know what the future holds for me...

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.160
But, I do know that once the season ends, 
I wanna come back to Portugal and look for some land of my own.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.920
And hopefully, some of the new friends I've met here, will come visit me.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.200
So, to start off with...

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.564
We're going to walk through the area,
and clear all of the small, thin, and dead mimosas.

00:04:09.564 --> 00:04:10.560
There's quite a lot of them.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.640
Clear the area up for us, so that we can work properly.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.400
Then, we'll move to the brambles.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.320
So that we definitely got a proper cleared area.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.480
And then, after that, we will start on stripping the mimosas.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.760
As Sean said, we are taking down the dead mimosas first,
and leave them on the ground.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.161
The principle will be that if they are stay up, 
they dry and are more prone to burn

00:04:31.411 --> 00:04:35.747
On the ground, they were rotten, 
so less fire risk and it helps to build some soil.

00:04:35.747 --> 00:04:39.840
So, it's kind of cool to walk into this area together,
 and collect all the dry sticks.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.040
The sounds are amazing!

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.360
- It is good, huh?

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.840
Super satisfying!

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.000
I feel powerful!

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.680
- Timber!

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.600
Look at this baby!

00:05:32.747 --> 00:05:34.680
We have to protect them now!

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.360
all of them.

00:06:51.236 --> 00:06:53.280
- Marilia, what do you have there?

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.680
Oh, I forgot the name in English.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.040
- Barbed wire?

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:57.600
What?

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.847
- Barbed wire!

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:01.874
Barbed wire.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.160
Barbed wire...

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.280
And look, they go down...

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.400
It's kind of dangerous.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.400
So, we have to watch out.

00:07:28.709 --> 00:07:33.760
Now that we've cleared the small, dead mimosas, 
we're going to mark out the area and measure it.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.200
So, we can see how much you've got
to do over the next few days.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.200
Now we know that this side is 25.6 meters.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.426
- How much?

00:08:30.425 --> 00:08:31.140
How much?

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.265
- 23 meters!

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.640
588 meters squared.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.434
- Half a hectare?

00:08:40.434 --> 00:08:41.790
Wait, no...

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:43.291
Not even close!

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.840
After clearing and measuring, we are placing the dry trunks,
to create water retention lines.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.640
This will tidy up the place,
help them rot and retain water.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.640
Which is good to build the soil over time.

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.760
Now, it's time to start stripping the bark of the mimosas.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.200
We also want to know how long it takes to debark this area.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.880
And, what tools are better for this task,
 so we can have useful information for research on how to deal with them.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.560
We don't need to take all the bark out.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.440
Just around one and a half meters from
the ground will be enough.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.400
It's kind of cool to see and to hear!

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.400
You have your own technique!

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.640
- The knife technique!

00:11:44.187 --> 00:11:46.547
It's going inside of your glove!

00:11:47.908 --> 00:11:49.083
Watch out!

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.609
All of the bark.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.520
This one is messy!

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:48.520
Yeah, some of the small ones are hard to get the bark off cleanly.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:51.946
Especially with this saw...

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.520
Well, we also could have a nice conversation?

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:11.160
- No, it's impossible, because everything you say, 
we put it in the video, and now I don't feel safe.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.567
Am I annoying you?

00:14:26.174 --> 00:14:27.133
Are you happy?

00:14:27.133 --> 00:14:28.382
- Very much!

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.720
These are the roots in the bark!

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.720
That's why we have to take it out, so they die.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:01.200
We just finished the debarking this big batch of mimosas.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:07.640
One of the advantage of the debarking 
is that the mimosas in theory shouldn't sprout.

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:14.280
Another advantage is that we can keep shade for a while,
and also it kind of looks cool!

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:17.320
But, there are some disadvantages as well.

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:20.880
For example, we have to debark them one by one.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:29.760
We only can do it in the spring, because in summer, 
the bark gets really stuck to the tree and it's quite difficult to take out.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:34.160
And, they took they take a long time to actually die.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:41.440
One other aspect is that we are not really sure 
if the thin ones should be debarked or not.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.699
We've used a number of tools
for working on them on the mimosas.

00:17:46.558 --> 00:17:48.899
I'll start the ones I wouldn't recommend...

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:50.560
The ax.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:53.760
Sometimes it's helpful, but you don't have enough fine control...

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.040
Maybe a bit too big for the job.

00:17:57.598 --> 00:17:58.588
The machete.

00:17:58.588 --> 00:18:00.118
Definitely too big for the job!

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.920
Like, you can maybe use it to cut around,
and get your initial cut in the wood.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.524
But, it just doesn't really work very well...

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.960
I've used this most of the time,
but again, I wouldn't really recommend it.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:22.800
With the sawtooth on the knife, 
it kind of is just hard to get behind the bark and peel it.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:26.040
Things that we've used the most:

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.320
All these secateurs!

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:34.320
The advantages are that they've got a relatively thin sharp blade.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:37.320
So, you can really kind of get behind the bark.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.520
But, as you can see, we've broken them.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:43.320
Yeah, they're stuck.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:46.480
We don't even need to put the latch on now.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.960
It's just stuck...

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:51.960
So, ideally, and we did order some of these...

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.481
But, it didn't arrive...

00:18:54.481 --> 00:18:56.792
It would be a knife, like just a small knife.

00:18:56.792 --> 00:18:58.478
This is Allie's knife that we've borrowed.

00:18:59.838 --> 00:19:05.640
A thin sharp blade can cut into the bark at the top pretty easily,
 and you can get in behind it and peel it off.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:09.520
So, if you're doing this yourself,
we'd recommend you small, sharp knife.

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:12.720
Okay, the debarking is done.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:18.934
But, before going to the other side of the patch, 
we will clear a small area below the electricity lines here

00:19:18.934 --> 00:19:20.560
Ale will explain the approach.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:24.297
So, we've reached a point right below that power line.

00:19:24.297 --> 00:19:31.160
Where the municipality cut all of their mimosas last year,
to keep it away from the power itself.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:34.560
And, you can see now, in one year,
how much they have grown!

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:41.600
It makes it a bit harder for us, because there's a lot of dead wood 
and stuff in the bottom, but also a lot of new growth...

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:45.320
What a mess!

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.037
- What a mess...

00:19:47.306 --> 00:19:49.018
Tricky situation!

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.324
So, here you can see what happens
when you only cut the mimosas.

00:20:50.324 --> 00:20:52.760
They just keep resprouting all over the place.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.000
We will have to work on it again next year, 
and probably in more seasons... 

00:20:58.823 --> 00:21:04.120
I would like to see how long do they take today, 
if we keep cutting them over and over.

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:10.200
We're now almost finished on the half of the mimosa patch,
that we we're debarking.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:11.864
We've got one job left there.

00:21:11.864 --> 00:21:15.329
We need to go and find of the little oaks and other native species.

00:21:15.329 --> 00:21:19.880
We've covered them with buckets to stop them from getting damaged,
and build them some proper triangles.

00:21:44.103 --> 00:21:46.457
Happy to be saving oaks?

00:21:46.457 --> 00:21:47.680
- Yeah!

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.400
Okay, so, the debarking area is finished!

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:00.880
It took us around 15 hours,
with the average of a team of five people.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.799
We debarked 500 trees!

00:22:04.085 --> 00:22:04.966
A lot!

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:09.569
But, this is not the only area 
that we are taking on in this mimosa patch.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:14.720
We have another site, but we're going to take it 
a bit different with a bit more power.

00:22:15.215 --> 00:22:16.335
With the same team!

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:24.000
Okay, we're now in the second half of the big mimosa patch,
with still much mimosa to go.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.028
This is also the area where the ruin is.

00:22:27.028 --> 00:22:29.802
Where we have the office,
which is where we need to clear all around.

00:22:30.231 --> 00:22:33.311
Again, we are starting we're making some space to work properly.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:36.400
Brushcutting, breaking and putting down all the dry sticks...

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.880
And, taking out the brambles,
so they don't pinch in the butt...

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:44.741
Woah! This is huge, Julie!

00:23:05.204 --> 00:23:07.733
We found the little lovely friend: the barbed wire...

00:23:07.733 --> 00:23:09.640
Always with us...

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:12.200
You never know where you might find it...

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:13.440
Right now, we are moving some dead mimosas,
taking out some brambles...

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:17.240
To make a safe area to work,
because it was kind of messy.

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:18.640
Now, it's beautiful!

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:23.120
Hey, guys?

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.760
We already cleaned the whole thing.

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:00.200
It's a nice area to work.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:04.053
Now, we can tear out the mimosas!

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:43.080
Okay, so, we only cut a couple of square meters, 
and we still have a lot to go...

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:48.760
But, we already have huge piles of green matter,
that we don't know what to do with.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:51.760
This is always a bit of a big problem...

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:57.009
But today, we're going to use one new shredder!

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:36.571
The shredder is cool for us,
 because the green matter stays on the land.

00:27:36.571 --> 00:27:38.360
It helps to protect and build the soil.

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:41.975
But, we can only shred the green tops
and the thin trunks.

00:27:41.975 --> 00:27:46.080
For the bigger ones, we will need to find a different solution.

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:19.960
So, we finished with the flat, easy part.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:25.080
It's now time to go into the steep and more challenging part
to start cutting the mimosas.

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:02.360
Okay, we realized during working on this steep part:

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.800
''It is better if we started down at the office.''

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:07.800
It's very hard and annoying,
to take the trees out from the top.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:11.040
But, if we start from the bottom,
we can dig them out easier.

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:24.240
I was using all my body weight to do it!

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:29.840
So, as you can see, we got a lot of mimosas!

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:31.280
Now, they are everywhere!

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:33.840
You cannot work, you cannot see the stairs...

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:38.200
So, now, it's time to process them,
and make this place look cleaner.

00:32:39.160 --> 00:32:40.818
- Processing party...

00:32:40.818 --> 00:32:42.160
Processing party!

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.920
So, we finished most of the mimosa cutting.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:24.520
Now, the last step is just cutting the last tree,
and then make everything a bit nicer.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:35.160
We have some brambles that we still have to move...

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:38.160
This pile of dead wood that we also 
have to spread and make nicer.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:41.240
Also, these trees are in the way of the office too much.

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:44.240
So, we are going to cut them, and make a nice path.

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:26.840
Now, the last tree!

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:55.960
All the chopping is done!

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:58.760
We have a lot of green matter to get rid of.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:02.397
Luckily, we have some help from our neighbor,
 who will take care of all the trunks.

00:35:02.934 --> 00:35:05.059
It would be better if we could keep them on the land.

00:35:05.059 --> 00:35:08.560
But, for the moment, we prefer to take them away,
 and reduce the fire risk.

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:12.680
As you might see, we are not used
to seeing such a big machinery over here.

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:13.960
Television!

00:36:03.400 --> 00:36:06.221
The camera adds pounds, doesn't it?

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:13.082
The camera adds extra pounds here, 
which is obviously beneficial...

00:36:13.082 --> 00:36:13.582
- A lot!

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:15.128
Yeah, I thought it might!

00:36:15.593 --> 00:36:17.265
- But, only to your biceps...

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:22.928
With all of the wood chips, that we got from the tops of the trees...

00:36:24.717 --> 00:36:29.800
We are going to use them to spread around the land,
that we just cleared to protect the soil.

00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:32.800
And also, to make the parts around the kitchen a little bit nicer.

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:24.905
So, as you can see, the piles are gone!

00:37:24.905 --> 00:37:27.120
From the surroundings, the area and the paths!

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:30.000
And now, the job is finally finished!

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:36.000
Looks nice!

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:36.866
Fluffy!

00:37:37.975 --> 00:37:40.975
I wish I was a fungus to live down here!

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:52.346
So, we've finished the experiment 
with this little part of mimosa on our land.

00:37:52.346 --> 00:37:54.560
By this section here, where we debarked it.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:57.480
And, this section here, where we got busy with the chainsaw.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:02.440
But, there's still loads more of mimosas on the land, 
so we just want to see which approach works best.

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:04.560
We're now in the debarked area.

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:08.080
It looks quite cool, although,
 maybe not so dramatic from the top, but...

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:11.080
You can see that we're already starting
to see some changes here.

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:13.040
The debarked area is changing color.

00:38:13.040 --> 00:38:15.600
You've got some resin coming out of the wood.

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:16.520
It's going to be an experiment.

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:19.680
Takes a couple of years, and we're looking forward 
to see how that turns out.

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:22.840
So, we're now in the section,
that we took out with the chainsaw.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:25.525
And yes, from down here, it does look ugly with all of the stumps...

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:27.785
We're going to do extra work here.

00:38:27.785 --> 00:38:31.200
We're going to plant some native species,
put down woodchips, improve the soil...

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:34.680
But, the reason we did it like this,
it's because we had to do it quickly.

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:40.000
The fire season is almost here,
 and the office was very close to all the trees!

00:38:40.215 --> 00:38:41.615
So, we needed to protect the office.

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:46.084
We're dealing with the woods in three different ways.

00:38:47.014 --> 00:38:48.334
The tops.

00:38:48.334 --> 00:38:49.400
We'll use those for wood chips.

00:38:49.400 --> 00:38:51.202
You can see this pile here is turning brown.

00:38:51.202 --> 00:38:52.905
We've already one big batch of wood chips!

00:38:52.905 --> 00:38:55.835
Someone will come with the shredder,
to help us with these again.

00:38:55.835 --> 00:38:58.555
And, the reason for that is that we
will use the woodchips to moisten the soil.

00:38:58.555 --> 00:39:00.930
Along the water lines is where we put all the dead wood.

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:03.164
That will help retain the water, when it rains.

00:39:03.164 --> 00:39:05.555
And, as they break down again, it will moisten the soil.

00:39:05.555 --> 00:39:08.553
The third thing is the big trunks.

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:13.032
The neighbor came with the truck,
and helped pick up load off of those.

00:39:13.032 --> 00:39:14.552
But, we've got more!

00:39:14.552 --> 00:39:16.960
And again, the neighbors are ever so helpful,
and they are going to come and take the rest.

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:19.426
You may be wondering why I've cut down all these trees.

00:39:19.426 --> 00:39:20.560
This is the reason!

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:23.960
The mimosa is a pioneer species,
but it's also an invasive species...

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:28.880
So, after the fire, they grow back really quickly
and they crowd out all of the native species, like the oak.

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:32.560
The native species are part of the ecosystem,
and they're also more fire resistant.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:34.793
So, we're going to promote the growth
of all of the little oaks

00:39:35.008 --> 00:39:39.240
And other native species or fruit trees,
and things that we found, so they can grow.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:45.080
With enough light and space, and hopefully we'll have a forest 
of all native species at some point in the future.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:46.520
So, our experiments here are done!

00:39:46.520 --> 00:39:49.840
And, we're hoping that that going to help inform how we do have all of the mimosas on the land.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:51.420
This is only a small patch!

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:53.028
And, not only for ourselves!

00:39:53.028 --> 00:39:56.720
We hope this research will help
other local people and people in the surrounding areas

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:01.320
To also help them deal with mimosas in the most efficient, quickest way,
as it's an invasive species in this region.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:04.880
But, for now, we're done
and we have this wonderful view!

